finish('fixed-parameter-tractability-versus-w1',
 id='TCS-6592', title='Is k-Clique fixed-parameter tractable, equivalently FPT=W[1]?',
 area='Parameterized and exact algorithms', criterion='resources', question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''Do there exist a total computable function f:ℕ→ℕ, a constant c, and one deterministic algorithm that decides whether an n-vertex graph contains a k-clique in time at most f(k)(n+2)ᶜ on every input? Equivalently, is FPT=W[1]?''',
 definitions=r'''An instance is a finite simple undirected graph G on [n], encoded by its adjacency matrix, and an integer k with 0≤k≤n, encoded in binary. A k-clique is a set of k distinct vertices for which every pair is an edge; k=0 is a yes-instance. Inputs with k>n are rejected immediately, and other malformed inputs are rejected. The parameter is k, not treewidth, degree, or the size of a supplied decomposition. Running time counts deterministic Turing-machine steps on the full input. Constants and the function f may be arbitrary but must be fixed independently of n; the exponent c must also be independent of k. FPT comprises parameterized decision problems solvable in f(k)Nᶜ time, where N is their input encoding length. One equivalent definition of W[1] is the class of parameterized problems reducible to k-Clique by a deterministic f(k)Nᶜ-time many-one reduction whose output parameter is bounded by a computable function of k alone. This is the standard parameterized reduction convention under which Clique is W[1]-complete. No randomness, advice, or promise about G is allowed.''',
 answer_criterion='Answer yes with such an algorithm for some computable f and fixed c. Answer no by proving that every deterministic exact Clique algorithm fails to satisfy every bound of this form. Proving a lower bound only under ETH or FPT≠W[1] does not resolve the class separation; finding an n^{g(k)} algorithm with unbounded g does not establish membership in FPT.',
 context=[
  paragraph('For each fixed k, exhaustive search over vertex subsets decides Clique in polynomial time. The exponent grows with k, so this observation gives a different kind of tractability: increasing the target clique size can increase the power of n in the running time. The question asks whether all dependence on k can instead be moved into one multiplicative function. For example, 2^{2^k}n³ meets the requested form, while n^{k/10} does not.'),
  paragraph('The unrestricted Clique problem is NP-complete, but this alone cannot rule out fixed-parameter tractability. The permitted function f can grow much faster than any polynomial when k grows with n. If P=NP then Clique is certainly FPT; the converse implication is not known. Thus FPT≠W[1] is a stronger separation conjecture than P≠NP in the direction currently proved.','structure'),
  paragraph('Parameterized reductions keep the output parameter controlled solely by the original parameter. Since Clique is complete under these reductions, an FPT Clique algorithm would solve every W[1] problem in FPT time. This is why a W[1]-hardness proof is useful evidence against a parameterized algorithm for another problem. The evidence is conditional on the separation being true, even when the reduction theorem itself is unconditional.','book'),
  paragraph('The Exponential Time Hypothesis supplies a stronger quantitative barrier: it rules out f(k)n^{o(k)} algorithms for Clique. That conclusion excludes FPT, but uses an assumption that is itself open. Matrix-multiplication methods and improved combinatorial methods accelerate Clique for fixed k while leaving an exponent that grows with the parameter. Their progress does not eliminate the central gap.','book'),
  paragraph('Approximation has become another way to measure the reach of the conjecture. Lin proves that even fixed-factor parameterized approximation of Clique is W[1]-hard. Later constructions strengthen the factor and simplify the reductions. These results enlarge the consequences of a separation; they do not prove FPT≠W[1].','lin')],
 why='This is the principal boundary question for algorithms whose exponential difficulty is confined to a small parameter. It determines the force of thousands of parameterized hardness reductions and separates efficient small-solution search from algorithms whose input-size exponent grows with the solution size.',
 importance=dict(score=99, method='editorial', assessed_on='2026-09-10', reason='The defining unresolved tractability separation of parameterized complexity, with Clique as a precise and widely used complete problem.'),
 progress=[
  step('1990s','Downey and Fellows develop the W-hierarchy and the completeness framework that makes parameterized Clique a canonical target for the FPT-versus-W[1] question.','book'),
  step('2000s–2015','ETH-based reductions establish the stronger conditional obstruction to f(k)n^{o(k)} Clique algorithms; the textbook develops this in its parameterized lower-bound chapters.','book'),
  step('2018','Bottesch studies structural and relativization evidence concerning FPT and W[1], explicitly distinguishing this conjecture from P≠NP. No unrelativized separation is proved.','structure'),
  step('2021','Lin proves constant-factor FPT approximation of Clique W[1]-hard, transferring the same central assumption to approximate search.','lin'),
  step('2025','Chen, Feng, Laekhanukit, and Liu give a simpler combinatorial construction for the k^{o(1)} parameterized Clique inapproximability bound.','approx'),
  step('2024–2026','Abboud, Fischer, and Shechter improve combinatorial fixed-k Clique running times by logarithmic factors. The Algorithmica publication of 22 May 2026 retains dependence on k in the exponent of n.','algorithm')],
 references=[
  ref('book','Parameterized Algorithms','Marek Cygan; Fedor V. Fomin; Łukasz Kowalik; Daniel Lokshtanov; Dániel Marx; Marcin Pilipczuk; Michał Pilipczuk; Saket Saurabh',2015,'https://www.mimuw.edu.pl/~malcin/book/parameterized-algorithms.pdf','Chapter 13, especially Sections 13.1 and 13.3; Chapter 14, especially Section 14.4.1; DOI 10.1007/978-3-319-21275-3'),
  ref('structure','On W[1]-Hardness as Evidence for Intractability','Ralph Christian Bottesch',2018,'https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.MFCS.2018.73','MFCS 2018, Article 73; Section 1, relations to P≠NP and ETH, and relativization results'),
  ref('lin','Constant Approximating k-Clique is W[1]-hard','Bingkai Lin',2021,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.04769','STOC 2021; gap reduction and constant-factor approximation consequence'),
  ref('approx','Simple Combinatorial Construction of the k^{o(1)}-Lower Bound for Approximating the Parameterized k-Clique','Yijia Chen; Yi Feng; Bundit Laekhanukit; Yanlin Liu',2025,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.07516','SOSA 2025, 263–280; DOI 10.1137/1.9781611978315.21'),
  ref('algorithm','Faster Combinatorial k-Clique Algorithms','Amir Abboud; Nick Fischer; Yarin Shechter',2026,'https://weizmann.elsevierpure.com/en/publications/faster-combinatorial-k-clique-algorithms-2/','Algorithmica 88(3), Article 48, 22 May 2026; DOI 10.1007/s00453-026-01390-8; earlier arXiv:2401.13502v2')],
 status='source_open', status_note='Explicitly presented as the central open conjecture in Bottesch’s primary paper and the parameterized-algorithms textbook. Research through 10 September 2026 located no proof of equality or separation. Conditional lower bounds and improved fixed-k algorithms are recorded with their distinct quantifiers.', formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
